\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{Short periods add winding terms}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{Start with all links equal to one on an #{L\times L} square
torus, then change one horizontal link to minus one. Keep the real
scalar convention, #{D=4+m^2}, and write
#{\Delta W=W(U_{\mathrm{flip}})-W(1)}.
Two adjacent plaquettes change sign, so
#{\Delta\sum_pU_p=-4}. For #{L\ge6}, the
[induced plaquette theorem](fdip-0072) gives
##{\Delta W=\frac4{D^4}+O(D^{-6}),\qquad
 \frac{\Delta W}{4/D^4}\longrightarrow1.}
The constants in the error bound may depend on the fixed volume.}

\p{For #{L=4}, there are also straight four-step walks winding
around a periodic row or column. The changed edge lies in one
horizontal winding cycle. It has 4 starting vertices and two
orientations, so 8 based walks change holonomy from one to minus
one. Their contribution to #{\Delta\operatorname{tr}H^4} is
#{-16}, in addition to the plaquettes' #{8(-4)=-32}.
There are no odd closed walks because this torus is bipartite.
The same convergent series therefore gives
##{\Delta W=\frac6{D^4}+O(D^{-6}),\qquad
 \frac{\Delta W}{4/D^4}\longrightarrow\frac32.}
The extra leading term is winding holonomy, not a different local
plaquette coefficient.}

\texfig{
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,
 vertex/.style={circle,draw,inner sep=2pt,minimum size=6mm}]
\node[vertex] (a) at (0,0) {$0$};
\node[vertex] (b) at (1.4,0) {$1$};
\node[vertex] (c) at (2.8,0) {$2$};
\node[vertex] (d) at (4.2,0) {$3$};
\draw[->,very thick] (a) -- node[above] {$-1$} (b);
\draw[->] (b) -- node[above] {$1$} (c);
\draw[->] (c) -- node[above] {$1$} (d);
\draw[->] (d) to[out=-90,in=-90,looseness=0.6]
 node[below] {$1$} (a);
\end{tikzpicture}
}

\p{The diagram shows one horizontal row of the #{4\times4} torus;
vertical edges are omitted. The curved edge is the periodic join.
The four-step winding product is minus one, independently of the
two neighboring plaquette products.}

\p{For #{L=5}, length-4 winding is absent, but each periodic
row or column has straight winding walks of length 5. The changed
row has 10 such based oriented walks, so
#{\Delta\operatorname{tr}H^5=-20}. No other five-step return can
wind: displacement 5 requires all 5 steps along that axis.
The plaquette change remains #{\Delta\operatorname{tr}H^4=-32}.
Consequently
##{\Delta W=\frac4{D^4}+\frac2{D^5}+O(D^{-6}).}
Thus a period-5 hypothesis would leave an order #{D^{-5}} term
outside the claimed order #{D^{-6}} remainder. This is why the
preceding theorem requires both periods to be at least 6.}
